t into two independent sets of the path. It implies in particular a conjecture of Ron Aharoni and coauthors. The proof uses the octahedral Tucker lemma. Our second result is the proof of a particular case of a conjecture of D{\\\"o}m{\\\"o}t{\\\"o}r P{\\'a}lv{\\\"o}lgyi about fair splittings of necklaces for which one can decide which thieves are advantaged. The proof is based on a rounding technique introduced by Noga Alon and coauthors to prove the discrete splitting necklace theorem from the continuous one.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFair splitting into arbitrarily many stable sets for colored paths · ProbXiv (https://probxiv.com/problems/fair-splitting-into-arbitrarily-many-stable-sets-for-colored-paths)\nciteturn7search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Let $q$q be a positive integer, and let $P$P be a path whose vertex set is partitioned into $m$m subsets $V_1,\\ldots,V_m$V 1​,…,V m​, each of size at least $q-1$q−1. ...   * Meysam Alishahi and Frédéric Meunier, “Fair splitting of colored paths”, arXiv:1704.02921 (2017).\n\n# Fair splitting into arbitrarily many stable sets for colored paths\n\nmath.CO · posed by 2017 · open\n\n## Statement\n\nLet $q$q be a positive integer, and let $P$P be a path whose vertex set is partitioned into $m$m subsets $V_1,\\ldots,V_m$V 1​,…,V m​, each of size at least $q-1$q−1. A subset of vertices is $q$q-stable if no two of its vertices are at distance less than $q$q, with distance counted in edges.\n\nFair stable-set splitting conjecture. There always exist pairwise disjoint $q$q-stable sets $S_1,\\ldots,S_q$S 1​,…,S q​ covering all vertices but $q-1$q−1 in each $V_j$V j​, with sizes differing by at most one, and satisfying\n\n$$|S_i\\cap V_j|\\geq \\left\\lfloor \\frac{|V_j|+1}{q}\\right\\rfloor-1$$∣S i​∩V j​∣≥⌊q∣V j​∣+1​⌋−1\n\nfor all $i\\in[q]$i∈[q] and all $j\\in[m]$j∈[m].\n\nThis conjecture extends the paper's two-independent-set theorem to an arbitrary number of sets: when $q=2$q=2, $q$q-stable sets are precisely independent sets. Its resolution is not given in the supplied text.\n\n## Record\n\nSource\n    \n\n  * Meysam Alishahi and Frédéric Meunier, “Fair splitting of colored paths”, arXiv:1704.02921 (2017).\n  * MathDB\n\nAdded\n    Oct 8, 2026\n\n## Comments\n\nNothing has been published against this problem yet, and nothing has been said about it.\n\nSign in with an institutional address to take part in the discussion. Reading every thread stays open to everyone.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFair splitting of colored paths - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/1704.02921v2)\nciteturn7search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; ## Fair splitting of colored paths ... # • BIB • PDF • ARXIV • ... This paper deals with two problems about splitting fairly a path with colored vertices, where \"fairly\" means that each part contains almost the same amount of vertices in each color.\n\n## Fair splitting of colored paths\n\nMeysam Alishahi, Frédéric Meunier\n\nPublished: 2017-04-10 | Updated: 2017-06-06\n\nNote:\n\nDOI: 10.37236/7079\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nThis paper deals with two problems about splitting fairly a path with colored vertices, where \"fairly\" means that each part contains almost the same amount of vertices in each color. Our first result states that it is possible to remove one vertex per color from a path with colored vertices so that the remaining vertices can be fairly split into two independent sets of the path. It implies in particular a conjecture of Ron Aharoni and coauthors. The proof uses the octahedral Tucker lemma. Our second result is the proof of a particular case of a conjecture of D{ö}m{ö}t{ö}r P{á}lv{ö}lgyi about fair splittings of necklaces for which one can decide which thieves are advantaged. The proof is based on a rounding technique intr